---
/**
 * PostBody — a saved post, whole, on its own page.
 *
 * `TweetCard.astro` shows a post the way a wall shows one: boxed, pressable,
 * and cut at 700 code points because a card holding 31,007 of them is a
 * fifteen-thousand-pixel column (`lib/post.ts`). **This is the other end of
 * that decision.** The card's ellipsis says there is more and the card's own
 * link is the way to it, and since VET-63 that link lands here rather than on
 * x.com, so this is where the rest has to actually be. No budget, no clamp, no
 * "show more": the whole post, set to be read.
 *
 * What that means in practice is the measure and the leading, because there is
 * nothing else to give it. **A post arrives flattened.** The pipeline reduces
 * the markdown Firecrawl returns to one run of text, so `Post.text` carries no
 * newline and the paragraph breaks the poster typed did not survive the trip.
 * Inventing them back — splitting on sentence endings, or on a length — would
 * be this page deciding where somebody else's thoughts stop, which is worse
 * than a long column. So it is one paragraph at the prose measure, and the day
 * the pipeline stops flattening, this is the page that shows the difference.
 *
 * The head is the name, the handle and the date the thing was posted. It is not
 * furniture repeated from the masthead above it — the `h1` is the post's title,
 * which is a clipped copy of these words, and the strip's date is the day the
 * link was saved. Who wrote it and when they wrote it are only here.
 *
 * ## The monogram left this page, and the reason is that it never belonged here
 *
 * **Aayush's review: "the letter instead of the profile photo looks kinda
 * odd".** It was a 48px hued circle holding one letter, the biggest coloured
 * object on a page whose other blocks open with an 11px label — standing in for
 * a face on a page that is not showing a face.
 *
 * Two answers were on the table and the ticket asked for a probe before
 * choosing. **Probed: Firecrawl's x.com post response carries no avatar at
 * all.** Two live scrapes of saved posts plus a schema-guided extraction came
 * back with the author's name, handle, date, text and `pbs.twimg.com/media/`
 * photos and nothing else; only the *profile* page carries `Profile Picture:
 * …pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/…`, which is a second scrape of a different URL
 * per author. So a real avatar is obtainable, and what it costs is a new
 * pipeline fetch for every post ever saved, a committed copy of somebody's face
 * with no honest date on it (design.md §6) that goes silently wrong the day they
 * change it, and a new class of rehosted image on /privacy.
 *
 * **So the monogram goes instead, and nothing replaces it**, because the slot
 * it was filling is not this page's slot. `TweetCard.astro` is a fallback
 * imitating a tweet, and the avatar is part of the thing it imitates; design.md
 * §3 already says this page is "a different rendering rather than a smaller
 * one" — the uncut post, set to be read. A reading page's head is a name, a
 * handle and a date, which is what every other block on this page opens with
 * and what the three of them already say without help. The identity was never
 * in the letter: `markFor` made the same call about a GitHub avatar in §5, and
 * for the same reason — a stranger's face at small size is not identity.
 *
 * Nothing outside this origin is named, the same claim `TweetCard.astro`
 * makes: the pictures are files in this repository because
 * `lib/library.ts › readShotPath` will not carry anything else.
 *
 * ## Closed under "Saved copy" since the embed went above it (VET-221)
 *
 * X's embed now sits above this block and shows the same words, so open it
 * would read as the page printing the post twice. A native `<details>`, closed
 * by default: the whole text is still in the HTML, so it is still the copy
 * that stays if the original is deleted, and one press shows it. With the
 * embed down, the fallback inside the blockquote already shows the words, so
 * closed is right in that state too.
 */
import type { LibraryEntry, Post } from "../lib/library";
import { formatDay } from "../lib/date";

interface Props {
  /** A post entry the pipeline could actually read. */
  entry: LibraryEntry & { post: Post };
}

const { entry } = Astro.props;
const { post } = entry;
---

<details class="post">
  <summary class="post__label mono">Saved copy</summary>
  <div class="post__head">
    <span class="post__author">{post.author}</span>
    <span class="post__meta mono">
      <span class="post__handle">@{post.handle}</span>
      <time class="post__posted" datetime={post.date}>{formatDay(post.date)}</time>
    </span>
  </div>

  <p class="post__text">{post.text}</p>

  {
    post.media.length > 0 && (
      <div class="post__media">
        {post.media.map((src, index) => (
          <img
            class="post__picture outlined"
            src={src}
            alt={
              post.media.length === 1
                ? `Picture attached to the post by ${post.author}`
                : `Picture ${index + 1} of ${post.media.length} attached to the post by ${post.author}`
            }
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        ))}
      </div>
    )
  }
</details>

<style>
  .post {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }

  /* The digest's label voice (`DigestBlocks.astro › .digest__label`), but it
     is a control, so `--text-secondary` rather than `--text-tertiary` (design.md §4, the ink
     floor) and 40px tall: 0.7rem above and below a 17.6px line. Left as a
     list item so the native disclosure marker stays; the focus ring is the
     global `:focus-visible` one. `fit-content` keeps the target and the ring
     to the words instead of the whole column. */
  .post__label {
    width: fit-content;
    padding-block: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.6;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition-property: color;
    transition-duration: var(--dur-fast);
    transition-timing-function: var(--ease);
  }

  .post__label:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* **A stack, where it used to be a row with a 48px circle at the head of it.**
     Nothing sits beside the name any more, so the horizontal flex and the gap
     that separated the two went with the monogram, and the inner `post__who`
     wrapper went with them — a column inside a row is one box when the row has
     one thing in it. This is the compactness the thebridge reference is mostly
     made of, taken where it was actually available: the head is 42px shorter
     and the name now starts at the column edge every other block on this page
     starts at. */
  .post__head {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    /* 0.2rem before, and the two lines are a name and its own metadata rather
       than two facts, so they close up. */
    gap: 0.15rem;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .post__author {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    /* -0.01em before. thebridge sets its titles at -0.3px on 14px, which is
       -0.021em, and design.md §4 adopted that reference's *density* while
       refusing its sizes: the tracking transfers where the size does not,
       because a size is a second label voice and a tenth of an em is not. */
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.3;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .post__meta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0 0.4rem;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
  }

  /* Drawn, never typed into the content: design.md §6. Keyed to the item after
     it, so a wrapped line starts with the separator instead of the line above
     ending on a dangling one. */
  .post__handle + .post__posted::before {
    content: "\00b7" / "";
    margin-right: 0.4rem;
    color: var(--hairline-strong);
  }

  .post__posted {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* The reading measure, which is the note page's and not the card's: 65ch at
     the site's prose size (design.md §2). The card sets a post at 0.9375rem
     inside a 550px box because it is a quote in a wall; a page holding
     thirty-one thousand characters of it is somebody reading, and the two want
     different type.

     `overflow-wrap` is for what a post is full of and a note page is not: a
     t.co shortlink has no word boundary in it, so without this one runs out
     past the column. */
  .post__text {
    /* 1.25rem before, when the head was a 48px circle and needed the air to
       clear it. The head is two lines of small type now, and it is this
       paragraph's byline rather than a block above it, so it closes up towards
       the 0.5rem every other block on this page puts between its label and its
       body without going all the way there — those labels are one line of 13px
       and this is a name over a date. */
    margin-top: 0.85rem;
    max-width: 65ch;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Stacked at the reading measure rather than in the card's 2-up grid. The
     grid is what fits four pictures inside a 550px card; here there is a whole
     column, and a picture given the width of the text is a picture you can see.
     One crop, 8:5, anchored to the top, which is the site's only answer for an
     image whose size nothing knows at build time (design.md §8). */
  .post__media {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    max-width: 65ch;
  }

  .post__picture {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 8 / 5;
    /* A flex item's automatic minimum height is its content, which overrules a
       ratio silently and has shipped here once already (design.md §8). */
    min-height: 0;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: top center;
    /* Sharp, like every other frame on the site. */
    border-radius: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 599px) {
    .post__text,
    .post__media {
      max-width: none;
    }
  }
</style>
